WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.850
Motifs, tapestry, amigurumi and Tunisian: crochet that sells.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.210
This is the video for module three of Advanced Knitting and Crochet.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:21.110
Crochet is fast and sturdy. This module covers four families that sell well. Joined motifs make blankets and bags. Tapestry crochet makes firm pouches.

4
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:31.420
Amigurumi makes stuffed toys, and Tunisian crochet makes scarves and dishcloths with a woven look. All instructions here use US terms.

5
00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:44.900
The skill is not making one square. It is making many squares the same size. In a classic granny square, round n has 4 times n clusters. If your count is different, the square will twist.

6
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:52.500
For example, Bisi in Akure makes one 4-round square, blocks it, and it measures 10 cm and weighs 9 g.

7
00:00:53.750 --> 00:01:03.480
The customer wants about 80 by 100 cm. That is 8 squares across and 10 down: 80 squares, and 720 g of yarn.

8
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:19.470
She adds about 15 percent for joining and the border, 828 g. In 50 g balls that is 16.6, so she buys 17, all from the same dye lot. Now she can quote a real yarn cost.

9
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.910
A slip stitch join gives a decorative ridge. A single crochet join is firmer, good for bags that take weight.

10
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:40.300
Whip stitch with a tapestry needle is flat, good for garments and baby items. Join-as-you-go attaches each square on its last round, which saves time on large blankets.

11
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:45.720
Block every square to the same size before you join, and photograph the layout first.

12
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:54.780
In tapestry crochet, the colour you are not using lies along the tops of the stitches, and you crochet over it, so it is hidden.

13
00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:04.930
To change colour, on the last stitch in colour A pull up a loop, so you have 2 loops on the hook. Then yarn over with colour B and pull through both.

14
00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:23.220
For example, Zainab in Kano has a diamond motif 12 stitches wide. Her flat base adds 6 stitches each round, so round 10 has 60. 60 divided by 12 is 5 diamonds. It fits, so no motif breaks where the round starts.

15
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:31.700
Amigurumi are small stuffed toys, worked in tight single crochet spirals with a marker at the start of each round.

16
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:39.330
Start with 6 stitches in a magic ring, and add 6 stitches every round: 12, 18, 24, 30.

17
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:48.690
Work plain rounds, then decrease 6 per round in the mirror order. Stuff firmly before the last round, and close it like a drawstring.

18
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.220
Stagger the increases each round, or the ball turns into a hexagon.

19
00:02:54.470 --> 00:03:04.410
Toy safety comes first. For babies and children under 3, embroider eyes and noses with yarn. Small parts that come loose are a choking hazard.

20
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:14.490
Use new, clean polyester toy stuffing, not old pillow filling or rags. Sew limbs and ears on through the body several times.

21
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:22.990
Give every toy a firm pull on each part before you sell or gift it, and avoid long cords that could wrap around a neck.

22
00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:36.790
Tunisian crochet uses a long hook and keeps many loops on it, like a knitting needle. On the forward pass you pick up loops. On the return pass you work them off, two at a time. You never turn the work.

23
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.650
Tunisian fabric curls. Fight it with a larger hook, a loose foundation chain, a border, and blocking.

24
00:03:45.900 --> 00:04:04.100
For example, Mercy in Benin City makes scarves of 225 rows. She times 10 rows at 25 minutes, so 2.5 minutes a row. With the border and blocking, each scarf takes about 10.5 hours. She will price from that real figure, not a guess.

25
00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:15.200
To remember. Weigh and measure one blocked square, then multiply. Make the round count a multiple of the motif. Add 6 stitches each increase round.

26
00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:23.600
Embroider features on toys for under-3s and pull-test every toy. And time yourself, so your prices use real hours.

27
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:36.000
Now plan a motif project and join two squares. Start a tapestry pouch from your own chart. Make and pull-test a sphere. And make a Tunisian swatch with a timing record.

28
00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.300
Continue with the lessons below.
